    "result_text": "Full suite is green. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented email-uniqueness enforcement on `POST /api/owners`, rejecting a create whose lower-cased email is already used by any other owner with **409 Conflict**. I mirrored the existing telephone-uniqueness pattern exactly, keeping each responsibility in its own small unit:\n\n- **`OwnerRepository.findByEmail(String)`** \u2014 new lookup on the interface, plus implementations in all three backends (`SpringDataOwnerRepository`, `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl`, `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl`). Each uses a case-insensitive `LOWER(email)` match so the rule holds against any stored casing.\n- **`EnsureUniqueEmail`** \u2014 new pipeline function that runs after `NormalizeOwnerEmail` (so it sees the lower-cased value) and before `BuildOwner`. Email is optional, so a blank email is left alone; a collision throws `DuplicateEmailException`.\n- **`DuplicateEmailException`** \u2014 checked exception (409 semantics), consistent with `DuplicateTelephoneException`.\n- **`DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler`** + escalation YAML \u2014 responds `409` with `{\"errors\":[\"email\"]}`, matching the telephone handler's shape.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `ensureUniqueEmail` step between `normalizeEmail` and `build`.\n\nNo existing class absorbed unrelated logic; each new piece parallels its telephone counterpart and reuses the normalization already done upstream.\n\nTest results: `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` and the full suite pass \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
